  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nAn omega block remains in place at 250 mb, albeit with a slightly \ndegraded eastern upper-level low. The CWA remains on the \neastern edge of this pattern nonetheless, and the ridge axis is \nset comfortably along a positive slope from Laredo, Texas, \nthrough to Lake Superior. Easterly progression of the ridge axis\nis expected to increase in speed by mid-day Saturday, though \nsome disagreement in the evolution of the western-most trough \nexists between models. Currently, the 12z GFS indicates a \nTuesday morning shortwave disturbance with a vort max over \nwestern Missouri. However, 06z ECMWF contains a strong zonal \ncomponent and a significantly weaker shortwave centered on \nwestern Iowa. Ensemble modeling (GEFS) remains more in line with\nthe ECMWF. Regardless of the trough\u2019s exact location, poor \nmoisture return from the Gulf will strongly inhibit the \npotential for severe weather, yet alone precipitation. Some\nsmall precipitation chances may return to the forecast Tuesday\nbut it is dry for now.\n\nNo notable changes to surface flow and conditions have occurred,\nand similar morning fog conditions will exist tomorrow morning\naround bodies of water. Any fog should erode with surface\nheating by 15z.",
